{
  "$schema": "https://aka.ms/codetour-schema",
  "title": "1. From GitHub issue to pull request",
  "description": "Follow one agent from the moment you assign it an issue to the pull request, the QA report and the merge. About 15 minutes.",
  "isPrimary": true,
  "steps": [
    {
      "title": "Welcome aboard",
      "description": "## Welcome aboard the USS Focus 🖖\n\nFocus is a 3D starship where a crew of **Claude Code agents** works through GitHub issues. This tour follows one agent end to end:\n\n1. you assign them an issue\n2. they get a station and a git worktree of their own\n3. a real Claude Code session works the issue (through the **Claude Agent SDK**)\n4. everything they do streams to the 3D office over a WebSocket\n5. the office pushes their branch and opens the pull request\n6. the room's QA officer tests it and posts a report\n7. a small pure function decides whether it can merge\n\nRun the demo in a terminal before you start, so you can watch it happen while you read:\n\n>> npm install && npm run demo\n\nThen open http://127.0.0.1:4477, click **Come aboard**, walk up to the board (**E**) and press **Assign agent** on an issue."
    },
    {
      "file": "server/index.ts",
      "pattern": "const runner = config\\.demo \\? new DemoRunner\\(\\) : new ClaudeRunner\\(browsers\\);",
      "title": "Demo mode and the real thing",
      "description": "### The same office, two crews\n\nThe server is put together here. In demo mode (`npm run demo`) the office swaps in a fake GitHub and scripted agents, and everything else (the manager, the board, QA, the 3D client) is the real code.\n\nThat's why the demo needs nothing set up, and it's a handy pattern for any agent app: the part that costs money and touches the world sits behind one interface (`AgentRunner`), and a fake that's good enough to build the UI against goes in its place."
    },
    {
      "file": "server/agents/manager.ts",
      "pattern": "^  hire\\(projectId: string, repo: string, issue:",
      "title": "Hiring an agent",
      "description": "### Assign agent → `hire`\n\nPressing **Assign agent** on the board ends up here. The agent is just a record at first: a name, a branch (`focus/<issue>-<slug>-<id>`), a desk in the project's holodeck and the status `queued`.\n\nNothing runs yet. Whether they start now is up to the scheduler."
    },
    {
      "file": "server/agents/manager.ts",
      "pattern": "^  schedule\\(\\): void \\{",
      "title": "Who starts work",
      "description": "### The scheduler\n\n`whoStarts` (in `schedule.ts`, with its tests next to it) decides who may start, given **Settings → Agents working at once**. Every Claude Code session uses your plan's limits, so the office keeps a cap on them.\n\nThose who may start are launched with `runFor`."
    },
    {
      "file": "server/agents/manager.ts",
      "pattern": "^  private runFor\\(id: string, work:",
      "title": "One fiber per agent",
      "description": "### Effect fibers\n\nThe manager runs on [Effect](https://effect.website/). Each agent's work is one **fiber** in a `FiberMap`, keyed by the agent's id, so an agent can never do two things at once.\n\nInterrupting the fiber (sending them home, or pressing Stop) interrupts their Claude Code session too, because the session is held in a `Scope`. That's why there's no hand-written cancellation code anywhere."
    },
    {
      "file": "server/agents/manager.ts",
      "pattern": "^  private launch\\(agent: Agent\\)",
      "title": "A worktree of their own",
      "description": "### Setting up\n\nBefore Claude starts, the office:\n\n- reads the issue from GitHub (`gh` CLI)\n- creates a **git worktree** for the agent on their new branch (`workspace.create`), so several agents can work in the same repo at once without stepping on each other\n- for a cloud agent, publishes the branch first, since the cloud session clones it from GitHub\n\nThen it hands over to `runSession`."
    },
    {
      "file": "server/agents/prompts.ts",
      "pattern": "^export function systemAppend\\(agent: Agent\\): string \\{",
      "title": "What the agent is told",
      "description": "### The instructions\n\nThis is appended to Claude Code's own system prompt. Look at the rules: commit as you go, run the tests, check UI changes in the browser, and **don't push or open a PR**. The office does that afterwards, so a session can never touch anything beyond its own branch.\n\nThe final message becomes the PR description, which is why it asks for a *Summary* and *Testing* section and no preamble.\n\nThe prompts are pure functions with tests in `prompts.test.ts`."
    },
    {
      "file": "server/agents/claudeRunner.ts",
      "pattern": "q = query\\(\\{ prompt: input, options \\}\\);",
      "title": "Claude Code, through the Agent SDK",
      "description": "### `query()`\n\nThis line starts a real Claude Code session, on your own Claude login, in the agent's worktree. Scroll up to see the options: the effort level, the permission mode (from the autonomy setting in the ready room), the browser MCP tools, and for a lead the office's own MCP tools.\n\n`input` is an async queue rather than a string, so your messages can reach the agent mid-turn. The loop below reads every message the session produces and hands it to `handle`, which turns it into terminal lines, browser frames, task lists and so on."
    },
    {
      "file": "server/agents/manager.ts",
      "pattern": "^  private hooks\\(agent: Agent\\): RunHooks \\{",
      "title": "From the session to the office",
      "description": "### Hooks\n\nWhat the runner sees comes back through these hooks: terminal lines, the browser's screenshots, the task list Claude keeps for itself, subagents (a lead's helpers sit down at their table), the cost so far.\n\nEach one updates the agent's record and sends an event to every open browser. Look at `permission`: in **ask** mode a risky command raises the agent's hand in the 3D office, and the Promise waits until you approve or refuse."
    },
    {
      "file": "server/hub.ts",
      "pattern": "^  send\\(event: ServerEvent\\): void \\{",
      "title": "Over the WebSocket",
      "description": "### The hub\n\nEvery event is a `ServerEvent` (defined in `shared/types.ts`, which the server and client both import) sent as JSON over `/ws`. Going the other way, the client calls REST endpoints under `/api` (`server/routes.ts`).\n\nA new tab gets a full `snapshot` first, then the stream of changes."
    },
    {
      "file": "client/src/net/state.ts",
      "pattern": "^      case 'agent':",
      "title": "The client's copy of the office",
      "description": "### Client state\n\nThe browser keeps its own copy of everything and emits a change event for each one. The panels and the 3D world listen for the parts they care about."
    },
    {
      "file": "client/src/world/room.ts",
      "pattern": "this\\.beams\\.push\\(new Beam\\(character\\.group, 'in'",
      "title": "Beaming in",
      "description": "### The 3D side\n\nWhen an agent turns up in the state, their room adds a cartoon crew member at their station, and they beam in: a column of sparkles that fades the figure in (`world/transporter.ts`).\n\nThe whole world is Three.js with toon materials and an outline pass. Their station's screen draws their live terminal and browser onto a canvas texture (`world/screen.ts`)."
    },
    {
      "file": "server/agents/manager.ts",
      "pattern": "^  private ship\\(agent: Agent, summary: string\\)",
      "title": "Shipping the work",
      "description": "### Push and open the PR\n\nWhen the session ends well, the office takes over:\n\n- uncommitted changes left behind? The agent is asked **once** to commit them (`COMMIT_NUDGE`)\n- no commits at all? They're done, with no PR\n- otherwise the office pushes the branch and opens the PR (or updates the one it already opened), with the agent's final message as the body\n\nThen it hands the PR to the room's QA officer."
    },
    {
      "file": "server/agents/qa.ts",
      "pattern": "^export const QA_SCHEMA",
      "title": "QA's structured report",
      "description": "### The QA officer\n\nQA is another Claude Code session, run by the same runner. It checks the PR out, reviews the diff, runs the tests and clicks through UI changes in the browser.\n\nThe difference is the output. Its last answer has to match this **JSON schema** (the Agent SDK's structured output), so the office gets a verdict, a list of checks and fix instructions it can act on, rather than prose. The office posts it on the PR as a comment, and QA itself never touches GitHub."
    },
    {
      "file": "shared/mergeGate.ts",
      "pattern": "^export function mergeStep\\(",
      "title": "Can it merge?",
      "description": "### Decisions are pure functions\n\nWith auto-merge on, this function decides the PR's next step: merge it, send it back to QA, re-run failed checks, hand it back to its developer, or wait.\n\nIt takes plain data and returns a plain answer, with no I/O, so `mergeGate.test.ts` can cover every case in a few milliseconds. The caller in the manager does the GitHub calls. That split runs through the whole codebase, and it's a large part of why agents can work on it safely: the risky decisions are small, readable and tested."
    },
    {
      "file": "server/agents/lead.ts",
      "pattern": "^export function shipPlan\\(",
      "title": "Bigger work: goals and their leads",
      "description": "### Leads\n\nIssues are for small jobs. For bigger work you start a **goal**: a long-lived lead session with every repo of the project checked out side by side, which builds it a slice at a time.\n\nA lead never pushes either. When a slice is ready, it calls the office's `mcp__office__ship` tool (`officeTool.ts`), and this pure function decides what that means: commit first, nothing to ship, or push these repos and open a PR in each."
    },
    {
      "file": "CLAUDE.md",
      "line": 1,
      "title": "How this repo is built",
      "description": "### The repo's own CLAUDE.md\n\nFocus is mostly built by Claude Code agents, often by Focus itself. This file is what every one of them reads first: the commands to run after each change, the conventions, and the rules for Effect code.\n\nIt's worth reading as an example of a CLAUDE.md that keeps a large, fast-moving codebase coherent.\n\nWhere to go next:\n\n- **docs/architecture.md** for the full map\n- `server/agents/claudeCrew.ts` for the read-only crew (the operations officer drafting issues, Number One)\n- `client/src/world/` for the starship itself\n- `docs/goals-plan.md` for how goals were designed"
    }
  ]
}
